 ### 3DGS Uncertainty Calculation via Fisher Information
-Computes an uncertainty attribute for 3D Gaussians based on the negative log-likelihood of the rendered pixel value, interpreted as Fisher information. It sets itself apart by providing an uncertainty map $\bar{C}_{V;G} = -\log p_f(\pi|V; G)$ for guidance. Choose this for uncertainty-aware rendering or active view selection. Link: [3DGS Uncertainty Calculation via Fisher Information](12937-3dgs-uncertainty-calculation-via-fisher-information.md) (inferred).
+Computes an uncertainty attribute $\bar{C}_{V;G} = -\log p_f(\pi|V; G)$ based on the negative log-likelihood of the rendered pixel [S14]. It assumes regularity conditions for volume rendering, such as differentiability and support independence [S14].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot M)$ per iteration, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $M$ is the number of Gaussians. The sorting step is $O(M \log M)$ per tile [6233-optimization-and-parallelization].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(M)$ to store Gaussian parameters. For SH Degree 1, each Gaussian requires $3 + 4 + 3 + 1 + 12 = 23$ floats [S3].
-*   **Measured Performance**: On a standard GPU, 30,000 iterations take several hours to days depending on scene complexity and resolution [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Fast training convergence, high-quality novel view synthesis, explicit representation allows for easy manipulation [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: High memory usage for large scenes, sensitive to initialization and hyperparameters (e.g., scene extent) [S4].
-*   **When to Use**: When high-fidelity novel view synthesis is required and computational resources are available for training.
-*   **When Not to Use**: When real-time training is required, or when memory is severely constrained without compression techniques [12943-data-compression-and-storage-optimization.md].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot T)$ per iteration, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians and $T$ is the number of tiles. Densification adds $O(N \log N)$ for sorting.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D)$, where $D$ is the dimensionality of Gaussian attributes (position, scale, rotation, opacity, SH coefficients). Half-precision fetches reduce register pressure [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Real-time rendering (30-100+ FPS) on modern GPUs is achievable due to the efficient rasterization pipeline [6233-optimization-and-parallelization].
+*   **Strengths:** High fidelity, real-time rendering, explicit representation allows for easy editing and compression.
+*   **Limitations:** High memory usage for large scenes; sensitive to initialization and hyperparameters (e.g., scene extent, pruning thresholds) [S4][S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When real-time novel view synthesis and high visual fidelity are required.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is severely constrained without compression, or when the scene has extreme dynamic range requiring specialized handling (e.g., HDR) not natively supported.
 
 ## Background
-
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1][S9, unofficial]. It builds on the idea of representing scenes as explicit Gaussian primitives, allowing for real-time rendering via differentiable rasterization. The method was developed to address the slow training and rendering speeds of NeRF-based methods [S9, unofficial]. The implementation details described here are based on the original paper and subsequent community implementations (e.g., gsplat) [S2, S3].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S2]. It builds on the concept of differentiable rasterization of 3D Gaussians, allowing for efficient optimization of radiance fields. The method has since been extended with various implementation details for densification, pruning, and acceleration, as documented in the sources [S1-S14].
 
